{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779301911,"nonce":"KrJojfnxknS0yKcH166Px5dW6M8=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABC5BBPg","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/145","origin":"github"},"title":"Concurrent Starlink + cell links with safety-critical traffic pinned to cell","message":"# Concurrent Starlink + cell links with safety-critical traffic pinned to cell\n\n## Problem\n\nToday's BizzyBoat comms architecture treats cell as a router-level WAN failover behind the RUTX11. Two problems with this in practice:\n\n1. **Blackout at WAN switch.** When Starlink drops, the router switches WAN, but the switch introduces a brief outage during which the operator loses all visibility into boat state — exactly when something potentially needs attention.\n\n2. **Cell connection becomes unreliable under high load.** Field observation across multiple deployments: when the cell link is carrying full traffic (post-failover, with everything redirected to it), it doesn't reliably stay up. The 2026-04-07 deployment documented WireGuard UDP 51820 being blocked on Verizon cellular with 0 bytes received boat→bencloud; the same period showed \"cell dropping API connections\" during high usage. The block was transient (worked the next day), and the standing mitigation was moving WireGuard to UDP 443 since \"carriers expect encrypted UDP on it.\" Working hypothesis (unverified): high-bandwidth UDP triggers carrier-side DPI / port-reassignment / filtering, and low-bandwidth UDP stays under whatever threshold causes that. If true, low-bandwidth traffic on cell should be stable; high-bandwidth on cell shouldn't be relied on.\n\nThe combination — brief blackout followed by an unreliable cell link under full load — means the \"everything on Starlink unless Starlink dies\" model is fragile for class operations where students need continuous awareness.\n\n## Proposed\n\nUse Starlink and cell **concurrently**, not as primary / fallback. Expose both as distinct routable paths and pin only a low-bandwidth always-on critical channel (heartbeat, position, `marine/command`) to a cell-backed udp_bridge connection. Bulk traffic (video, costmap, diagnostics) stays on Starlink as today.\n\nThis sidesteps both problems:\n- No WAN switch means no blackout.\n- Cell carries only steady low-bitrate traffic, never bulk — which aligns with the \"low-bandwidth stays under the carrier-filtering threshold\" hypothesis. If the hypothesis holds, the cell link should stay reliable indefinitely; if it doesn't hold, we learn something testable about what the actual mechanism is.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is primarily a networking-architecture question, not a software one:\n\n- **Router config (Teltonika RUTX11)**: cell exposed as a distinct addressable path independent of WAN failover. Likely means a dedicated cellular APN/route rather than the existing failover stack.\n- **VPN topology**: may need a second VPN endpoint reachable specifically via cell, depending on how the existing operator-side VPN routes return traffic. The 2026-04 finding that WireGuard on UDP 443 survives where 51820 doesn't is directly relevant — any cell-side VPN endpoint should be on 443.\n- **udp_bridge config**: small. The bridge already supports per-topic routing per `topics_list` per connection (see existing `wifi` / `vpn` split in `bizzyboat_project11/config/bizzyboat.yaml`). Adding a third `cell` connection with a narrow `topics_list` of safety-critical topics is a yaml edit once the network path exists.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Is a separate VPN required, or can the cell path share the existing endpoint via a different routing rule?\n- Does the cellular plan / APN tolerate continuous low-bitrate UDP traffic without throttling? (Test directly — current observations cover the high-bandwidth case but not the sustained-low case.)\n- What's the right topic set for the cell channel? `marine/heartbeat`, `marine/status/mission_manager`, `mavros/global_position/raw/fix`, `marine/command` are the obvious candidates; the full list deserves bandwidth-budgeting before commit.\n- How does this interact with the over-horizon framing? OOL operations could also benefit — cell often outlives Starlink near horizon, or vice versa.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nLake Massabesic survey (June 15+) puts student operators in the loop. Brief blackouts at WAN switch are noticeable to a student trying to follow the mission — they may interpret the outage as \"boat lost,\" queue manual interventions during the actual recovery, or trigger false alarms. The unreliable-cell-after-failover problem turns \"the fallback should save us\" into \"the fallback works briefly then struggles\" — students operating without expert intuition will not recognize the degraded-link signature in real time. Continuous awareness via a cell channel sidesteps the class of failure entirely.\n\n## Related\n\n- [`rolker/udp_bridge#19`](https://github.com/rolker/udp_bridge/issues/19) — per-topic priority/class scheduling (orthogonal: prioritization within a link vs. assignment across multiple links).\n- 2026-04-07 / 2026-04-08 WireGuard-on-Verizon observations documented in `docs/bizzyboat_deployment_log.md`.\n- 2026-05-19 deployment observations of Starlink-only behavior ([`#135`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/135)).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- udp_bridge code changes — the per-topic-per-connection routing already exists.\n- Pre-June-4 work — this is post-survey / maintenance-mode unless the network side comes together quickly.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}